-- #1111 A-2.6, drains #1259 (S1, import-spelling-dependent FALSE REJECT): two
-- unrelated modules each declare a constructor spelled `Dup`, and this unit imports
-- `A1` and its `Dup` BY NAME plus `zmodd`'s unrelated `zdf`.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE capture: `Dup` here can only mean
-- `amodd.Dup : Int -> A1` — the only `Dup` this file brings into scope — so `v : A1`
-- agrees with it, `unA1 v` is `5`, and `zdf 1` is `1`.
--
-- Pre-fix (verified first-hand on a binary built from 40756bea) this exact file was
-- REJECTED with `Type mismatch: A1 vs Z1`, naming a type it never mentions, while the
-- SAME program spelled `import amodd.{A1(..)}` was accepted.  That spelling split is
-- the tell: both spellings bring the same `Dup` into scope, so the defect was the
-- bare-name keying of the ctor env plus an overlay that reached only `(..)`, not
-- whether the right `Dup` was visible.
--
-- 🚨 This is the `exit 1 → exit 0` direction, so the new acceptance is untested by
-- construction — no pre-existing fixture could fail on it.  The mirrors that must
-- STILL be rejected are pinned as
-- `test/analyze_project_fixtures/1111_a26_genuine_ctor_rejects/` (no collision) and
-- `.../1111_a26_collision_rejects/` (collision present).
import amodd.{A1, Dup, unA1}
import zmodd.{zdf}

v : A1
v = Dup 5

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString (unA1 v))
  putStrLn (intToString (zdf 1))
